# autosave writes here every 2 min; tracking it makes the loop trigger itself

# exemplar audio: large, recoverable from the D2L zip

# Raw filing downloads inside run-research folders, added 2026-09-01.
# Agents cache the source 10-K/10-Q/DEF 14A while working. Those are large (Ford's FY2025
# 10-K alone is 5 MB) and ~70 runs remain in the queue, which would add hundreds of MB to a
# 532 MB history that cannot be pruned without a rewrite. They are RE-FETCHABLE FROM EDGAR,
# and the provenance requirement under operator rule 4 is the recorded document, date and
# ACCESSION NUMBER in the run file, not a copy of the document in version control.
# Existing tracked research is deliberately left alone; this prevents FUTURE additions only.
# ONE LEVEL DEEP WAS NOT DEEP ENOUGH, found 2026-09-11: the SNPS run's fetcher wrote its raw
# filings under a cache/ subfolder, and every rule below matched only the folder's top level.
# 28 MB of re-fetchable .htm entered history in a single preserve commit (909dd68). The
# patterns are now recursive. That commit stands - history is not rewritten here - and the
# files are removed from the tree going forward with `git rm --cached`.
Test Runs/_research*/**/*.htm
Test Runs/_research*/**/*.html
Test Runs/_research*/**/*.pdf
Test Runs/_research*/**/*.xlsx
Test Runs/_research*/**/cache/

# STRIPPED-TEXT FILING DUMPS, added 2026-09-07 after the DELL run reported that the rules
# above cover .htm/.pdf/.xlsx but NOT .txt - so every agent that strips a filing to text
# bypassed them silently. A stripped 10-K is the same category as the .htm it came from:
# large, re-fetchable from EDGAR, and not the provenance record (operator rule 4 wants the
# accession number in the run file, not a copy of the document in version control).
#
# DELIBERATELY PATTERN-MATCHED RATHER THAN A BLANKET *.txt. Of 400 tracked research .txt
# files sampled, 190 are over 200KB and are plainly filing dumps - but 76 are under 20KB and
# are analysis notes. A blanket rule would silently swallow a future run's working notes,
# which is a worse failure than a large repo. The names are strongly patterned, so the
# patterns are what is ignored.
#
# This prevents FUTURE additions only; the 1,192 already-tracked .txt files are untouched,
# because .gitignore does not untrack anything.
Test Runs/_research*/**/*10-K*.txt
Test Runs/_research*/**/*10K*.txt
Test Runs/_research*/**/*10k*.txt
Test Runs/_research*/**/*10-Q*.txt
Test Runs/_research*/**/*10Q*.txt
Test Runs/_research*/**/*10q*.txt
Test Runs/_research*/**/*DEF14A*.txt
Test Runs/_research*/**/*DEF_14A*.txt
Test Runs/_research*/**/*def14a*.txt
Test Runs/_research*/**/*8-K*.txt
Test Runs/_research*/**/*8K*.txt
Test Runs/_research*/**/*20-F*.txt
Test Runs/_research*/**/*20F*.txt

# OVERNIGHT AUTOMATION, added 2026-09-12. The prompt and the cycle script are committed; the lock
# (which holds a live PID) and the per-cycle logs (full session transcripts) are not.
Screens/_daily/_overnight.lock
Screens/_daily/_overnight_logs/*
!Screens/_daily/_overnight_logs/.gitkeep
Screens/_daily/_wake_body.md

# XBRL COMPANY-FACT DUMPS AND UN-PATTERNED STRIPPED FILINGS, added 2026-09-21 by the MGM run,
# which committed 25.7 MB of them before noticing. The rules above ignore stripped filings by NAME
# PATTERN (*10-K*.txt, *10-Q*.txt and eleven more) and their own comment says the names are
# "strongly patterned". The MGM run's were not: it wrote tenk_2025.txt, tenq_2026Q2.txt,
# *_body.txt and companyfacts.json, and every one of them bypassed all thirteen patterns. There is
# also NO rule at all covering the SEC XBRL companyfacts/submissions JSON, which every run in this
# tree downloads and which runs 1.4-4.0 MB per registrant - the MGM run pulled eight of them for
# its competitor row. All are RE-FETCHABLE FROM EDGAR and none is the provenance record: operator
# rule 4 wants the document, date and ACCESSION NUMBER in the run file, which is where they are.
# The 25.7 MB already committed in f3f51b5 and 4af9913 STAYS IN HISTORY - it is recorded, never
# rebased (operator rule 6) - and the files are removed from the tree going forward with
# `git rm --cached`. This prevents FUTURE additions only.
Test Runs/_research*/**/companyfacts.json
Test Runs/_research*/**/*companyfacts*.json
Test Runs/_research*/**/submissions.json
Test Runs/_research*/**/*submissions*.json
Test Runs/_research*/**/tenk*.txt
Test Runs/_research*/**/tenq*.txt
Test Runs/_research*/**/ten-k*.txt
Test Runs/_research*/**/ten-q*.txt
# _body.txt is this run's own naming convention for "the filing stripped to text". Named
# explicitly rather than as a blanket *.txt, for the same reason the block above gives: a blanket
# rule would silently swallow a future run's working notes, which is a worse failure than a large
# repository.
Test Runs/_research*/**/*_body.txt

# MORE UN-PATTERNED FILING DUMPS, added 2026-09-21 by the AGCO run. The MGM block above
# caught tenk*.txt / tenq*.txt / *_body.txt / companyfacts.json; it did not catch two more
# shapes this run produced. (1) A PEER dump named after the peer and its fiscal year -
# peers/DE_fy2025.txt (683 KB) and peers/CNH_fy2025.txt (558 KB) - which matches none of the
# sixteen patterns above, because "10-K" appears nowhere in the name. (2) The SEC SUBMISSIONS
# feed saved as subs.json, where the block above ignores only submissions.json and
# *submissions*.json. Both are re-fetchable from EDGAR and neither is the provenance record
# (operator rule 4 wants the accession number in the run file, which is where it is). Named by
# pattern rather than by a blanket rule, for the reason the 2026-09-07 block gives: a blanket
# *.txt would silently swallow a future run's working notes.
Test Runs/_research*/**/*_fy20*.txt
Test Runs/_research*/**/*_FY20*.txt
Test Runs/_research*/**/subs.json
Test Runs/_research*/**/*_raw.json


